`com.sun.net.httpserver` isn't resolvable — because the Android unit test classpath uses `android.jar` mockable + JDK modules; the `jdk.httpserver` module isn't on the compile classpath for the Android unit test compilation (AGP restricts the JDK modules? Actually, unit tests compile against the mockable android.jar and the JDK's bootclasspath; `com.sun.net.httpserver` is in the `jdk.httpserver` module, which IS part of the JDK but not in the default `java.se` root set for compilation? For javac/kotlinc, the default root modules include all java.* and jdk.* modules exported by java.se... `jdk.httpserver` is a JDK module that is not in java.se, so it isn't resolved by default when compiling with `--release` or the module path.

Options:
1. Add `--add-modules jdk.httpserver` to the Kotlin compile options for unit tests — complicated.
2. Use a plain `ServerSocket`-based fake server (java.net.ServerSocket is in java.base, always available). Writing a minimal HTTP/1.1 server with ServerSocket is straightforward: read the request line + headers, read the body per Content-Length, write the response.

Option 2 is portable and has no build changes. Let me write a small test helper `FakeHttpServer` in the test sources that serves canned responses over a raw socket. It needs to handle:
- Read the request line and headers until CRLFCRLF.
- Read the body by Content-Length.
- Respond with "HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\nContent-Type: ...\r\nContent-Length: N\r\n\r\n" + body, and close the connection (Connection: close). HttpURLConnection handles that fine.

Also for the "slow chunked stream" cancellation test, I need to write the response in two parts with a delay. With Connection: close and Content-Length, I can write the first part, flush, sleep, then write the rest. HttpURLConnection will read the first bytes as they arrive (it uses a fixed-length stream; reading returns what's available? BufferedReader.readLine() blocks until data or EOF... Actually `readLine()` on a socket InputStreamReader returns as soon as it has a complete line, so the first "data: ..." line will be delivered immediately. Good.)

So a `FakeHttp` helper:

```kotlin
class FakeHttp(private val responder: (requestBody: String) -> List<Pair<String, String>>) { ... }
```
Simpler: a class that takes a list of `Resp(contentType, body, status)` and serves them in order (repeating the last), with an optional `pauseAfterFirstWriteMs`.

Let me write it as a shared test helper file: `android/app/src/test/java/dev/pocketrun/agent/FakeHttpServer.kt` in package dev.pocketrun.agent (and import it from the opencode test package).

```kotlin
package dev.pocketrun.agent

import java.io.BufferedReader
import java.io.InputStreamReader
import java.net.ServerSocket
import java.net.Socket
import java.util.ArrayDeque
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean

/**
 * A one-shot HTTP server on a loopback port: enough of HTTP/1.1 to answer the
 * LLM client's requests without pulling in a server library. Android unit tests
 * compile against a stub android.jar, so only java.base is available.
 */
class FakeHttpServer(responses: List<FakeHttpServer.Resp>) {
    data class Resp(val contentType: String, val body: String, val status: Int = 200, val pauseMs: Int = 0)

    private val queue = ArrayDeque(responses)
    private val server = ServerSocket(0, 0, InetAddress.getByName("127.0.0.1"))
    private val running = AtomicBoolean(true)
    val port: Int get() = server.localPort

    init {
        thread(isDaemon = true, name = "fake-http") {
            while (running.get()) {
                try {
                    server.accept().use { handle(it) }
                } catch (e: Throwable) { if (running.get()) continue else return@thread }
            }
        }
    }

    private fun handle(socket: Socket) {
        socket.use { s ->
            val input = BufferedReader(InputStreamReader(s.getInputStream(), Charsets.ISO_8859_1))
            val requestLine = input.readLine() ?: return
            var length = 0
            while (true) {
                val line = input.readLine() ?: break
                if (line.isEmpty()) break
                val name = line.substringBefore(':').lowercase()
                if (name == "content-length") length = line.substringAfter(':').trim().toInt()
            }
            val body = CharArray(length)
            var read = 0
            while (read < length) {
                val n = input.read(body, read, length - read)
                if (n < 0) break
                read += n
            }
            val resp = synchronized(queue) { if (queue.size > 1) queue.removeFirst() else queue.first() }
            val bytes = resp.body.toByteArray(Charsets.UTF_8)
            val head = buildString {
                append("HTTP/1.1 ${resp.status} ${if (resp.status == 200) "OK" else "Error"}\r\n")
                append("Content-Type: ${resp.contentType}\r\n")
                append("Content-Length: ${bytes.size}\r\n")
                append("Connection: close\r\n\r\n")
            }
            val out = s.getOutputStream()
            out.write(head.toByteArray(Charsets.ISO_8859_1))
            if (resp.pauseMs > 0 && bytes.size > 64) {
                // Send the first part, wait, then the rest: a stream that stalls.
                out.write(bytes, 0, 64)
                out.flush()
                Thread.sleep(resp.pauseMs.toLong())
                out.write(bytes, 64, bytes.size - 64)
            } else {
                out.write(bytes)
            }
            out.flush()
        }
    }

    fun close() { running.set(false); runCatching { server.close() } }
}
```

Note: HttpURLConnection may send "Expect: 100-continue"? No, only if configured. It sends Content-Length for a byte array body. Also it may use HTTP/1.1 with keep-alive; we respond with Connection: close, which is fine.

One concern: for the 401 case, my client reads `conn.errorStream`. With Content-Length set and status 401, HttpURLConnection gives an error stream. Good.

Another: `LlmClient` sets `Accept: text/event-stream, application/json` — irrelevant.

The BufferedReader reading of the body: the body is JSON with UTF-8; I read it as ISO_8859_1 into chars — the length in bytes vs chars could differ, but I only need to consume it, and I decode the body nowhere. However, reading N bytes as ISO-8859_1 gives N chars 1:1. Good.

Careful: `input.read(...)` may return fewer; loop handles it.

Now update both test files to use FakeHttpServer. Also, since the AGP unit test runtime classpath is java.base only, `java.util.Locale` etc. are fine.

Let me write the helper and update the tests.

